{"answer":"The critic's case concedes the target's own stated concessions and then overstates what those concessions establish.\n\nFirst clause: The target does propose a skeptical-defeat pre-order and acceptance test, and concedes that reinstatement becomes primitive rather than derived. But the critic's claim that this 'fails the stated standard even on the problem's own terms' is not actually established by the conceded facts alone. The target's concession acknowledges a cost; it does not concede a violation of an explicit problem requirement unless the problem independently and unambiguously mandates that reinstatement be derived. The target never claims to satisfy that mandate; the critic asserts the mandate, but the artifact under review does not establish its precise wording. Conceding a cost is not conceding a requirement violation. The defender does not grant that the problem text has been proven to contain that exact requirement as the critic interprets it.\n\nSecond clause: The target already concedes that committed-root validity may break on mixed cyclic/acyclic graphs. The defender grants only what the target itself grants: a risk on mixed graphs. The critic's stronger claim, that the pre-order 'can' rank a defeated attacker strictly below its target and thereby cause acceptance despite an undefeated defeater, is not established by the target's text; the target flags the issue as criticizable, not as demonstrated. No concrete mixed-graph counterexample is established in the record, and the defender does not concede one. The committed-root validity concern stands as an open, explicitly acknowledged risk, not as a proven break.\n\nAccordingly: the reinstatement concern is conceded only as a cost the target already acknowledges, not as a proven failure of an established requirement; and the committed-root concern is conceded only as an acknowledged risk, not as a demonstrated violation. Nothing further is established, and the defender concedes nothing beyond the target's own admissions."}